1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,545
This Python file has five mistakes.

2
00:00:03,595 --> 00:00:07,030
Run it, and Python tells
you about exactly one.

3
00:00:08,467 --> 00:00:12,175
If you are coming from C,
C plus plus, Java or PHP,

4
00:00:12,225 --> 00:00:14,607
your hands already know a syntax.

5
00:00:14,657 --> 00:00:20,577
Python will reject some of it, crash on
some of it, and silently accept the rest.

6
00:00:21,100 --> 00:00:25,332
So we will sort the mistakes
by when you find out.

7
00:00:25,382 --> 00:00:27,815
One: Python refuses to start.

8
00:00:27,865 --> 00:00:30,641
Two: it fails when the line runs.

9
00:00:30,691 --> 00:00:33,210
Three: it says nothing at all.

10
00:00:34,333 --> 00:00:37,009
Start with the one Python did report.

11
00:00:37,059 --> 00:00:42,756
In C or PHP, parentheses and braces mark
the block. In Python, the colon does.

12
00:00:42,806 --> 00:00:47,323
Leave it out, and nothing in the
file runs. Not even line one.

13
00:00:48,467 --> 00:00:49,822
Braces are gone too.

14
00:00:49,872 --> 00:00:52,632
In Python, the indentation is the block.

15
00:00:52,682 --> 00:00:56,988
One line that does not line up,
or a tab mixed in with spaces,

16
00:00:57,038 --> 00:00:59,377
and again the file will not start.

17
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:04,362
The rest of this group are
the same kind of habit.

18
00:01:04,412 --> 00:01:06,689
Double ampersand becomes and.

19
00:01:06,739 --> 00:01:11,183
Plus plus becomes plus equals
one. Else if becomes elif.

20
00:01:11,233 --> 00:01:14,393
The three part for loop becomes a range.

21
00:01:14,443 --> 00:01:19,770
And null becomes None, with a
capital N. But look at that last one.

22
00:01:19,820 --> 00:01:21,776
It is not a syntax error.

23
00:01:21,826 --> 00:01:26,430
The file starts, and fails
later. That is the second kind.

24
00:01:27,867 --> 00:01:29,080
Back to our file.

25
00:01:29,130 --> 00:01:34,802
Add the colon, and run it again. No
error. But no output either. Nothing ran.

26
00:01:34,852 --> 00:01:39,261
That silence is the third kind,
and we will come back to it.

27
00:01:39,311 --> 00:01:43,497
For now, the fix is two
underscores on each side of main.

28
00:01:44,433 --> 00:01:45,209
Run again.

29
00:01:45,259 --> 00:01:48,924
Now the file starts,
and crashes inside push.

30
00:01:48,974 --> 00:01:52,143
In C, you write into an array by index.

31
00:01:52,193 --> 00:01:56,023
In PHP, assigning to an
index creates the slot.

32
00:01:56,073 --> 00:01:59,903
A Python list never grows
that way. You append.

33
00:02:00,833 --> 00:02:05,000
Run again. Thirty, twenty. It
works. And this is the trap.

34
00:02:05,050 --> 00:02:09,870
Two mistakes are still in the file,
in lines that have not run yet.

35
00:02:09,920 --> 00:02:14,014
A C compiler checks every line
before the program starts.

36
00:02:14,064 --> 00:02:16,703
Python checks a line when it runs it.

37
00:02:17,233 --> 00:02:18,888
So make those lines run.

38
00:02:18,938 --> 00:02:21,587
Pop a third time, from an empty stack.

39
00:02:21,637 --> 00:02:25,423
Type error: exceptions must
derive from BaseException.

40
00:02:25,473 --> 00:02:28,122
In C plus plus you can throw anything.

41
00:02:28,172 --> 00:02:32,383
In Python, an exception class
has to inherit from Exception.

42
00:02:33,333 --> 00:02:33,951
Run again.

43
00:02:34,001 --> 00:02:39,490
The except branch runs for the first time,
and finds a name that was never defined.

44
00:02:39,540 --> 00:02:43,227
One wrong letter, sitting
there since the first version.

45
00:02:43,277 --> 00:02:45,763
Fix it, and the file is finally clean.

46
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:52,352
The same kind shows up everywhere.
Lowercase true. Calling length as a method.

47
00:02:52,402 --> 00:02:54,327
Adding a number to a string.

48
00:02:54,377 --> 00:02:56,302
Forgetting self in a method.

49
00:02:56,352 --> 00:03:00,394
Dividing with one slash, and
using the result as an index.

50
00:03:00,444 --> 00:03:04,062
All of them start fine, and
fail when the line runs.

51
00:03:04,112 --> 00:03:07,730
So the rule is: make every
branch run at least once.

52
00:03:09,767 --> 00:03:13,844
Now the third kind: no error
at all. Remember the silence?

53
00:03:13,894 --> 00:03:19,323
The name is compared with the wrong
string, so the condition is simply false.

54
00:03:19,373 --> 00:03:21,031
Python has no complaint.

55
00:03:21,081 --> 00:03:23,237
Your program just does nothing.

56
00:03:24,267 --> 00:03:26,135
Plus plus i is legal Python.

57
00:03:26,185 --> 00:03:28,395
It is a plus sign, applied twice.

58
00:03:28,445 --> 00:03:31,752
The value does not change,
and nothing warns you.

59
00:03:31,802 --> 00:03:35,657
As the counter of a while
loop, that is an infinite loop.

60
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:41,438
Range one to five stops at
four. The end is never included.

61
00:03:41,488 --> 00:03:44,846
And five divided by two is two point five.

62
00:03:44,896 --> 00:03:47,930
For integer division, use two slashes.

63
00:03:48,967 --> 00:03:50,928
Assignment does not copy.

64
00:03:50,978 --> 00:03:56,960
In PHP, and with a C plus plus vector,
b equals a gives you a second array.

65
00:03:57,010 --> 00:04:00,580
In Python, both names
point at the same list.

66
00:04:00,630 --> 00:04:03,637
Change one, and the other changes too.

67
00:04:04,667 --> 00:04:08,584
And a default argument is
created once, not once per call.

68
00:04:08,634 --> 00:04:14,057
Give a function an empty list as a default,
and every call shares that one list.

69
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:17,998
Two things that are not mistakes.

70
00:04:18,048 --> 00:04:20,987
A semicolon at the end of a line is legal.

71
00:04:21,037 --> 00:04:23,691
So are parentheses around a condition.

72
00:04:23,741 --> 00:04:29,170
Some lists online call them syntax
errors. They are not. They are just noise.

73
00:04:30,300 --> 00:04:34,118
Put the three kinds side by
side, and there is a pattern.

74
00:04:34,168 --> 00:04:38,665
The mistakes you make most often
are the ones Python catches first.

75
00:04:38,715 --> 00:04:42,330
The ones that cost you hours
are the rare, quiet ones.

76
00:04:43,467 --> 00:04:44,580
So, two habits.

77
00:04:44,630 --> 00:04:47,372
Run the file after every small edit.

78
00:04:47,422 --> 00:04:52,646
And make every branch run at least
once. Python will tell you a lot.

79
00:04:52,696 --> 00:04:56,137
But only about the lines
it has actually run.

80
00:04:57,767 --> 00:04:59,011
Thanks for watching.

81
00:04:59,061 --> 00:05:03,607
Subscribe to Edge Cases for more
Python, algorithms, and system design,

82
00:05:03,657 --> 00:05:04,837
explained visually.
